A||||3||Predator||Question for Hydrolic Clutch diagnose||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||12-06-2001||08:21 AM||||Hey,<P>I posted a while back about my clutch being spongy and not wanting to go into gear on my 85 gt manual after the car gets warmed up. I tried bleeding the hydraulic clutch, but it did not fix the problem. Also, since the master cylinder has never been open to my knowledge how could air get into the lines unless gaskets have gone bad. My next step is to determine if the master or slave cylinder is bad. Can anyone tell me if there is an easy way of determining this? Also, is it better to try and rebuild them or replace them, and how big of a job is it? Thanks in advance for the help..<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||tesmith66||12-06-2001||09:31 AM||||First, look at the clutch arm. If it is stamped steel with the rubber insert, it is cracked. Replace it with a cast iron arm. I got mine at a dealer. These arms are weak and are almost always the culprit on the 4 speed.<P>On the slave cylinder, pull back the rubber boot covering the pushrod and see if there is any fluid visible. Sometimes they leak enough that they coat the inside of the boot with fluid. Also check both ends of the hydraulic hose at the fittings and then the hose itself. Also check the bleeder valve for leaks.<P>Then inspect the hard line all the way up to the master cylinder. Look for leaks and/or cracks, especially at mounting points.<P>At the master cylinder, check for fluid residue on the firewall at the base of the cylinder and at the fitting. Then go under the dash and look for leaks coming from the back of the cylinder where the pushrod goes in. While your down there, look at the pushrod to make sure it is straight, and installed with the loop on top.<P>The clutch pedals can bend, as well. Check yours with a magnet. If it is steel, you're OK. If it's the original aluminum, it is either bent or is going to bend. If, after everything else, it still won't work correctly, replace the pedal.<P>If you are not losing fluid, and it appears to get worse as the engine warms up, then it is probably the release arm. If it does it all the time, hot or cold, then it is probably the pedal and/or pushrod (banjo) under the dash.<P>Sorry I got windy, but I have had a lot of first hand experience in ALL of these areas, and I want to make sure you don't miss anything. I ended up replacing every component (from clutch pedal pad to clutch disk) before I could declare it fixed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||12-06-2001||11:04 AM||||Mine always got air in but didn't lose any fluid.  Turned out to be gunk in the slave causing the piston to not seal properly.  I don't know about the master, but the slave is very easy to rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||12-06-2001||01:25 PM||||I had trouble with my 1984 SE clutch when I first got the car. The slave cyl shaft would creep back if you held the pedal all the way down. Also, I could only get about 1" or maybe a little less of travel.<P>I replaced the MC and the clutch pedal and bled it according to the instructions on V8<BR>Archie's web site. Then I could get about 1-1/8" of travel and it didn't creep back if I held the pedal to the floor.<BR>||||reg||1
